WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
in this episode of XXfiles:

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.980
If we can gain a glimpse of what their health status is

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.460
just by taking a small piece

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.240
of skin and looking at these microbes

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.460
then that may aid our understanding of

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.780
their health and ultimately that's what

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.400
we need for their conservation.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.040
Wooh! Humpback!

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:23.800
We have a humpback too.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:26.640
Oh there's a couple up here.

00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:34.339
Little dolphin.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.640
Oh, here he is.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.450
Wooh!

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.020
My name is Amy Apprill and i'm an assistant scientist

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.220
at the Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:15.710
I've been studying the skin microbiome

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:17.590
of the humpbacks for several years now.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.480
And we started to pick apart some

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.520
trends in this microbiome.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.290
A microbiome is basically just an assemblage

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:26.930
of microorganisms.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:28.960
These are mostly single cells

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.900
that live in association with a marine animal.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:33.980
We've been able to look at

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:36.170
several health compromised whales.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:38.180
That either have been stranded on beaches

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:40.100
or entangled in fishing line and what

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:41.630
we found is that their microbiome is

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:43.610
shifted compared to the healthy animals.

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:45.830
And so they don't have these same what

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:47.450
we think are potentially helpful groups

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:50.120
of bacteria on their skin and these

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:52.430
health compromised animals also have started

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:55.510
to develop relationships with potential pathogens.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:57.220
So if we can gain a glimpse

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:58.520
of what their health status is

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:00.800
just by taking a small piece of skin and

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.810
looking at these microbes then that may

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:04.850
aid our understanding of their health

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:06.380
and ultimately that's what we need for

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:07.410
their conservation.

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:08.570
We're going to look at the microbes

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:09.880
in two different ways

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:11.660
One way as we look at who the microbes

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:14.090
are using their DNA as well as look at

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:16.430
where the microbes are under the microscope.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:20.680
It's been really exciting to bring this

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:23.870
new way of looking at the microbiome

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.640
to a community of marine mammal scientists

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.190
that are already so invested in learning

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.080
more about the health of these animals

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.760
and the measures that are needed to

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.340
conserve the species.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:37.440
I'm working with Jooke Robbins who's a senior scientist

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.060
at the Provincetown Center for Coastal Studies.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:43.300
Jooke's been fantastic and getting us skin samples

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:45.140
that we can take into my lab

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.220
and start to analyze and look at at the microbes.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:50.440
Do you see him David?

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:51.080
No.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:03.890
Ugh, that might have been a dive which means

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:05.730
we may not see him for a few minutes.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:10.160
Yesterday we were out on the tip of Cape Cod

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.350
cod chasing humpback whales.

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:14.270
We were lucky enough to obtain a skin biopsy sample

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:16.250
from one of these humpback whales.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:17.690
We then spend a little bit of time

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:20.150
cleaning up the samples and then we send

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:21.810
them off to a sequencing facility.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.860
Usually within a couple weeks we receive our data back.

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:28.000
The sequence data that we get back  is just letters.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:29.980
This is the DNA code.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:31.910
In every sample that we've looked at

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:33.950
from basically around the world

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:35.250
we find two main groups of bacteria:

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:41.860
This is a really powerful finding.

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:44.480
That we find wales living in really different

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.280
ocean environments that have

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.260
the same types of bacteria on their skin.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:49.880
And then whales do this other thing that

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:51.860
I think is really cool is that they shed

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:53.900
their skin pretty frequently and so

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.150
they're constantly somehow reviving that

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:57.490
population of microbes.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:00.470
We think there must be some other type of mechanism

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:02.690
that allows these cells to maintain

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:05.300
residence on the skin surface.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:07.730
The whale skin is so complex and that there are so

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:09.290
many cells there that it's really

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:11.180
challenging to pick apart what is a microbial cell.

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:15.180
So the way that we're going about that is

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:19.600
we're using a DNA strand that's specific for bacteria

00:04:19.609 --> 00:04:21.859
and it has a fluorescent tag on the end.

00:04:21.859 --> 00:04:24.999
And we add that to these little thin sections

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:26.500
and allow it to sit overnight

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:29.690
and then what we'll do is look at it

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:31.790
under the microscope and where we see

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:34.010
that fluorescent tag should correspond

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:37.890
to where the bacterial cells are.

00:04:38.729 --> 00:04:41.879
Oh yeah there's the edge.  That's cool.

00:04:41.879 --> 00:04:43.949
So here we're looking at the surface of the

00:04:43.949 --> 00:04:46.169
skin layer and under the microscope we

00:04:46.169 --> 00:04:48.360
see the structure of the the skin

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.789
in white here in contrast to the black

00:04:50.789 --> 00:04:52.229
where there's no sample what we're

00:04:52.229 --> 00:04:54.240
looking for some some bright spots where

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.669
probe and we do see a few of those on

00:04:56.669 --> 00:04:58.830
the surface right in that area

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:01.169
yeah there's another chain cells over

00:05:01.169 --> 00:05:03.419
there and potentially some deeper as well.

00:05:03.419 --> 00:05:05.520
Once we look at more samples

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.199
we'll be able to form a better understanding

00:05:07.199 --> 00:05:09.820
of how these cells are associating with the whale skin.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:15.120
Oh my god look, it just surfaced.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:16.120
Right over there.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:18.820
So we've been following this juvenile humpback whale

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:20.760
for several minutes now.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:22.300
Thus far we don't recognize it as a whale

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:23.620
that we've catalogued before

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:25.020
nor as a whale that we

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.050
biopsy sampled before and that's why it's

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:29.460
important for us to get a sample today

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:32.220
to apply to a number of different studies.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.949
Jen is poised with a dart biopsy and

00:05:34.949 --> 00:05:36.990
what Jen's is going to be doing here is

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:38.789
hopefully when the whale comes up to the

00:05:38.789 --> 00:05:41.089
surface and the boat is at the right angle

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:42.839
and close enough to the animal

00:05:42.839 --> 00:05:44.610
Jen is going to release that dart and

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:47.990
get a skin biopsy from from the whale

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:54.860
Alright.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.330
Nice work Jen.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:00.070
Let's take a sample.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.720
The dart has a float that will let it remain

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:05.500
at the surface until we're able to pick it up with the net.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.060
Being able to scale up

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.630
from this relationship that we're studying in the lab

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:15.960
to actually seeing these animals

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:18.360
in the wild is really important.

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:20.550
We're also interested in expanding our

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.970
studies to more species of marine mammals.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:25.280
So we've studied about 16 species

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:28.340
thus far and there's about 129 species

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:30.480
of marine mammals out there.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:33.740
We're finding new kinds of life everyday

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:36.830
and these microbiomes on marine mammals

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:39.490
haven't been studied in any capacity in the past.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.580
So it's allowing us to discover a new relationship.

00:06:42.889 --> 00:06:45.469
I can't believe how many new collaborations

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:46.540
I've been able to spin up

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:48.700
just in the last few years and so I

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:50.990
think this is a really good sign

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:52.400
and a promise that we're going to be able to

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:55.560
continue this research.

